{
  "paragraphs": [
    {
      "index": 0,
      "blockIndex": 0,
      "paragraphInBlock": 0,
      "text": "Knowing the lens's radius of curvature $R$ lets us calculate the radii of the dark Newton rings.\nLight of wavelength $\\lambda$ cancels at certain places, making those places dark."
    },
    {
      "index": 1,
      "blockIndex": 0,
      "paragraphInBlock": 1,
      "text": "The air gap between the lens and the flat glass plate has the same thickness all around each circle centered on their contact point.\nThat is why the dark places form rings."
    },
    {
      "index": 2,
      "blockIndex": 0,
      "paragraphInBlock": 2,
      "text": "We first consider the ideal case in which the lens and plate truly touch at the center.\nIf a small gap remains there, the central brightness and dark-ring radii may change, so the true-contact results cannot be applied directly."
    },
    {
      "index": 3,
      "blockIndex": 1,
      "paragraphInBlock": 0,
      "text": "For the dark ring with order $k$, call its radius $r_k$ and the air gap's thickness there $h_k$.\nThe right triangle in Figure 200 connects these quantities."
    },
    {
      "index": 4,
      "blockIndex": 1,
      "paragraphInBlock": 1,
      "text": "The Pythagorean theorem gives:\n$R^2=r_k^2+(R-h_k)^2$.\nExpanding gives $2Rh_k=r_k^2+h_k^2$."
    },
    {
      "index": 5,
      "blockIndex": 1,
      "paragraphInBlock": 2,
      "text": "The lens's radius of curvature is much larger than the wavelength and $h_k$.\nThe gap is very thin, so $h_k\\ll r_k$."
    },
    {
      "index": 6,
      "blockIndex": 1,
      "paragraphInBlock": 3,
      "text": "The squared thickness is tiny compared with the squared ring radius, so we neglect it and get $2Rh_k\\approx r_k^2$.\nThe approximate thickness is:\n$h_k\\approx\\frac{r_k^2}{2R}$ (7.6)."
    },
    {
      "index": 7,
      "blockIndex": 4,
      "paragraphInBlock": 0,
      "text": "Compare the light reflected from the opposite sides of the thin air gap.\nThe wave reflected from the glass plate below travels an extra distance $2h_k$ through the air, going down and back up."
    },
    {
      "index": 8,
      "blockIndex": 4,
      "paragraphInBlock": 1,
      "text": "When it reflects from air onto glass, its phase also changes by $\\pi$: its oscillation is reversed.\nThe wave reflected at the lens's underside meets the air boundary from inside the glass."
    },
    {
      "index": 9,
      "blockIndex": 4,
      "paragraphInBlock": 2,
      "text": "It does not have the same reflection reversal there.\nThe lower reflection's phase change is equivalent to an extra path difference $\\frac{\\lambda}{2}$, not extra distance actually traveled."
    },
    {
      "index": 10,
      "blockIndex": 4,
      "paragraphInBlock": 3,
      "text": "This explains the source footnote's warning: travel through the gap alone does not give the full path difference used for interference.\nHere the wavelength is the wavelength in the air gap, approximated as the vacuum wavelength as in the source calculation."
    },
    {
      "index": 11,
      "blockIndex": 4,
      "paragraphInBlock": 4,
      "text": "In the ideal limit of true contact at the center, the air gap has zero thickness, so there is no extra round trip through air.\nThe phase reversal on reflection still makes the reflected waves cancel, giving a dark center in the reflected pattern."
    },
    {
      "index": 12,
      "blockIndex": 4,
      "paragraphInBlock": 5,
      "text": "A dark ring depends on the phase difference from both travel and reflection.\nUsing the interference-minimum condition from Section 46:"
    },
    {
      "index": 13,
      "blockIndex": 4,
      "paragraphInBlock": 6,
      "text": "$2h_k+\\frac{\\lambda}{2}=\\frac{\\lambda}{2}(2k+1)$,\nwhere $k=0,1,2\\cdots\\cdots$."
    },
    {
      "index": 14,
      "blockIndex": 5,
      "paragraphInBlock": 0,
      "text": "Substitute the expression for the air gap's thickness $h_k$ from (7.6) into the dark-ring condition.\nThe wavelength $\\lambda$ and the lens's radius of curvature $R$ then determine the dark ring's radius $r_k$:\n$r_k=\\sqrt{k\\lambda R}$ (7.7)."
    },
    {
      "index": 15,
      "blockIndex": 5,
      "paragraphInBlock": 1,
      "text": "This relation describes the reflected Newton rings above: a very thin gap, nearly perpendicular light, and the same reflection-phase relationship.\nThe zero-order dark region has zero radius: it is the central dark spot, not a ring of finite radius.\nTo measure wavelength using this radius relation, choose a dark ring outside the center and maintain true contact at the center."
    },
    {
      "index": 16,
      "blockIndex": 6,
      "paragraphInBlock": 0,
      "text": "Measure a dark ring's radius and identify its order.\nIf you also know the lens's radius of curvature, the relation just found lets you determine the wavelength."
    },
    {
      "index": 17,
      "blockIndex": 7,
      "paragraphInBlock": 0,
      "text": "The source reports $\\lambda_{\\text{红}}=8\\times10^{-5}$ centimeters for red light and $\\lambda_{\\text{紫}}=4\\times10^{-5}$ centimeters for violet light.\nIt uses these values to describe the wavelengths of the other spectral colors as lying between them (see color plate V; source citation: 彩图 V)."
    },
    {
      "index": 18,
      "blockIndex": 7,
      "paragraphInBlock": 1,
      "text": "These are the source's reported values, not precise boundaries defining red and violet.\nAll visible-light wavelengths are very short."
    },
    {
      "index": 19,
      "blockIndex": 7,
      "paragraphInBlock": 2,
      "text": "The source compares them with sea waves whose average wavelength is a few meters.\nImagine enlarging such a sea wave until it stretches across the Atlantic from the American coast to the European coast.\nEnlarge a light wave by the same factor, and its wavelength would still be only a little wider than a page of this book."
    },
    {
      "index": 20,
      "blockIndex": 8,
      "paragraphInBlock": 0,
      "text": "Interference shows that light behaves as a wave and gives us a way to measure its wavelength.\nFrequency distinguishes the pitch of a sound."
    },
    {
      "index": 21,
      "blockIndex": 8,
      "paragraphInBlock": 1,
      "text": "The color of monochromatic light is also related to frequency;\nwithin the same medium, wavelength can distinguish its color."
    },
    {
      "index": 22,
      "blockIndex": 8,
      "paragraphInBlock": 2,
      "text": "In the source's relation $\\lambda\\nu=c$, wavelength times frequency gives the speed of light in vacuum.\nThe wavelength in this relation is therefore the vacuum wavelength.\nAfter changing the medium, we must not substitute its wavelength while still using the vacuum speed."
    },
    {
      "index": 23,
      "blockIndex": 9,
      "paragraphInBlock": 0,
      "text": "Color is a sensation produced when our eyes receive light;\nlight itself has different wavelengths."
    },
    {
      "index": 24,
      "blockIndex": 9,
      "paragraphInBlock": 1,
      "text": "The source gives about $10^{-6}$ centimeters as a small wavelength difference the eye can distinguish.\nThat is the source's estimate, not the same limit for every color and viewing condition."
    },
    {
      "index": 25,
      "blockIndex": 9,
      "paragraphInBlock": 2,
      "text": "The source also claims that most animals see only black and white.\nThat is not an accurate general rule: animals differ in their ability to distinguish colors."
    },
    {
      "index": 26,
      "blockIndex": 10,
      "paragraphInBlock": 0,
      "text": "Light's wavelength changes when it enters another medium.\nFill the gap between the lens and glass plate with water or a transparent liquid of refractive index $n$ instead of air."
    },
    {
      "index": 27,
      "blockIndex": 10,
      "paragraphInBlock": 1,
      "text": "For such liquids that keep the reflection-phase relationship described above, the interference rings become smaller.\nKeep the light source and lens unchanged when comparing the rings, so the change shows the medium's effect."
    },
    {
      "index": 28,
      "blockIndex": 11,
      "paragraphInBlock": 0,
      "text": "Why do the rings shrink after adding the liquid?\nWhen light enters the transparent medium considered here from vacuum, refractive index $n$ describes the factor by which its speed decreases."
    },
    {
      "index": 29,
      "blockIndex": 11,
      "paragraphInBlock": 1,
      "text": "Speed, wavelength, and frequency in the medium still satisfy $v=\\lambda\\nu$.\nThe frequency stays unchanged, so the wavelength becomes $\\frac{1}{n}$ of its vacuum value."
    },
    {
      "index": 30,
      "blockIndex": 11,
      "paragraphInBlock": 2,
      "text": "The ring-radius relation must therefore use the wavelength in the gap's medium, not the vacuum wavelength.\nThe source's statement that wavelength changes by a factor of $n$ means that it becomes shorter, not longer."
    },
    {
      "index": 31,
      "blockIndex": 11,
      "paragraphInBlock": 3,
      "text": "The frequency stays unchanged, so entering another medium alone does not change the light into another color.\nWith the reflection-phase relationship unchanged, a shorter wavelength gives smaller dark rings."
    }
  ],
